    "question": {
      ":question/id": "c61702be-b22f-5790-aa4e-f6200453551f",
      ":question/problem": "Express\n\n$$\n\\cos^{2}\\left(\\frac{8\\pi}{13}\\right)-\\sin^{2}\\left(\\frac{8\\pi}{13}\\right)\n$$\n\nas a single trigonometric ratio.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Recall the **double-angle formula for cosine**:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 x-\\sin^2 x=\\cos(2x).\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=\\frac{8\\pi}{13}$ and double the angle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\left(\\cos^{2}\\left(\\frac{8\\pi}{13}\\right)-\\sin^{2}\\left(\\frac{8\\pi}{13}\\right)\\right) \\\\\n&=\\cos\\left(2\\cdot \\frac{8\\pi}{13}\\right) \\\\\n&=\\cos\\left(\\frac{16\\pi}{13}\\right).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnly the angle doubles; the common coefficient stays outside the cosine.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
